<!-- PROCESS DEPTH -->
<section class="cx-problem cx-section" id="process-depth">
  <div class="cx-container">
    <div class="r">
      <div class="cx-eyebrow">What Each Step Actually Involves</div>
      <h2 class="cx-h2">Not a tagline. A real workflow.</h2>
      <p class="cx-sub" style="max-width:620px">Every contractor has a process slide. Here is what ours actually looks like in practice — the specifics that make the difference between a smooth project and a painful one.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cx-problem-grid stagger">
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>The Site Visit</h3>
        <p>Before a single number goes into a quote, we walk the space. We are looking at base building condition, MEP penetrations, existing slab, coordination items with building management, and anything the drawings might not show. The quote reflects what is actually there — not what should be there.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>The Itemized Quote</h3>
        <p>Our quotes are broken out by trade, with scope clearly defined and exclusions explicitly listed. No allowances buried in line items. No "as per drawings" that covers unknown conditions. If something is uncertain, we say so — and we tell you how we have priced for it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>Long-Lead Order Management</h3>
        <p>On day one of the project, we identify every item with a lead time longer than two weeks — millwork, specialty lighting, flooring, hardware — and place those orders before demolition begins. That is how the reception desk arrives on the day it is needed, not three weeks after.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>Trade Sequencing</h3>
        <p>We build a master trade sequence before the first truck rolls. Demo, framing, MEP rough-in, inspections, insulation, drywall, MEP trim, ceilings, flooring, millwork, paint, hardware. Every trade knows when they are starting, what comes before them, and what they are handing off to.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>Daily Site Accountability</h3>
        <p>One person owns the site schedule — not a rotating cast of site supervisors. They know where every trade is against plan, what is on the critical path today, and what action is required to protect the next milestone. That clarity is what prevents the domino delay.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-problem-card r">
        <h3>The Punch List Close</h3>
        <p>We do a full internal walkthrough before the client walkthrough. On a 3,500 sq ft project, that typically generates 15–30 items. 80% close same-day. The remaining 20% close within 48 hours. We do not hand you a 60-item list and disappear to the next job.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- PROCESS COMPARISON -->
<section class="cx-proc-compare cx-section" id="process-compare">
  <div class="cx-container">
    <div class="r">
      <div class="cx-eyebrow">VNG vs. The Typical GC</div>
      <h2 class="cx-h2">How the process compares<br>when something goes wrong.</h2>
      <p class="cx-sub" style="max-width:600px">The real difference between contractors does not show up when everything goes well. It shows up when the permit comes back with a comment, or a trade falls behind, or a site condition changes the scope. Here is how VNG handles it versus the industry norm.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cx-proc-cols r">
      <div class="cx-proc-col cx-bad">
        <div class="cx-proc-col-label">Typical GC Approach</div>
        <h3>What usually happens</h3>
        <ul class="cx-proc-list">
          <li>Trade falls behind — PM finds out at end of week when next trade shows up and leaves</li>
          <li>Change in site condition becomes a verbal "that is extra" with no formal documentation</li>
          <li>Permit delay stops all work and timeline clock resets</li>
          <li>Punch list presented at handoff with 50+ items, crew has moved to next job</li>
          <li>No structured update — you call the site to find out what is happening</li>
          <li>Millwork ordered late because nobody tracked the lead time at project start</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-proc-col cx-good">
        <div class="cx-proc-col-label">The VNG Approach</div>
        <h3>What we do instead</h3>
        <ul class="cx-proc-list">
          <li>Trade slippage identified same day — resequencing begins immediately to protect milestone</li>
          <li>Every scope change documented in a formal change order with cost and schedule impact before work proceeds</li>
          <li>Permit delay anticipated — permittable and non-permittable work resequenced to keep other trades moving</li>
          <li>Internal punch list walk done before client walkthrough — 80% of items closed same-day</li>
          <li>Structured weekly update sent regardless of whether anything notable happened that week</li>
          <li>All long-lead items ordered on day one against calculated delivery dates</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

